column2089:
Jan 30, 2019 16:30;615b3e9cc5388b00696ca529;As we cannot apply any policy under an interface used for DMVPN, and the bandwidth changed to 20M instead of 100k, there are 3 policy(6M+6M+4M) under the tunnel265 which is causing the global output drops, so the last thing we changed is the replay window size(by default it is 64k) to 1024.

var01.hkg15#sh log | inc jmalpas
Jan 30 08:25:08 GMT: %SYS-5-CONFIG_I: Configured from console by jmalpas on vty0 (63.223.136.10)
Jan 30 08:30:27 GMT: %CLEAR-5-COUNTERS: Clear counter on interface TenGigabitEthernet2/1/0 by jmalpas on vty0 (63.223.136.10)

Let's monitor again now.

{color:#000000}var01.hkg15(config-if)#service-policy output Cisco-Test{color}

{color:#000000}Service_policy with queueing features on this interface is not allowed {color}

{color:#000000}if dmvpn based queuing policy is already installed.{color}

{color:#1f4e79}Hi Jean,{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}Thank you for sharing your findings and configuration change with me.{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}I was reviewing the logs sent by you earlier and could see anti-replay error on the router{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}Jan 22 08:03:07 GMT: %IOSXE-3-PLATFORM: F0: cpp_cp: QFP:0.1 Thread:137 TS:00022500072833740335 %IPSEC-3-REPLAY_ERROR: IPSec SA receives anti-replay error, DP Handle 182, src_addr 63.216.140.170, dest_addr 63.218.210.7, SPI 0xab79eaa0{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}This is a replay error and it could be one of the reason for packet drops as per the following document [{color:#1f4e79}https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/internet-key-exchange-ike/116858-problem-replay-00.html#anc3{color}] {color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}By default, the window size is fairly small (window size of 64).{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}The replay window size can be increased by using the following command globally:{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}crypto ipsec security-association replay window-size 1024{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}Kindly note that it doesn’t take the affect immediately and we will have to wait for a day to check if the drops still persist. Also, post configuration change do clear counter as well.{color}

 

{color:#1f4e79}Thanks & Regards,{color}

{color:#1f4e79}Rehan Haider{color}

{color:#1f4e79}{color:#1f4e79}ENGINEER.CUSTOMER SUPPORT – Enterprise Core Architecture
 +rhaider@cisco.com+{color}{color}

column2091:
Feb 06, 2019 02:17;615b3e9cc5388b00696ca529;Last email from Cisco TAC is available below but the configuration Cisco wants to apply is not supported.

Cisco TAC advised, Waiting for Cisco TAC feedback now.

{color:#1f4e79}Explanation of why we see drops in class:-{color}{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}The class showing the drops may be experiencing microbursts. Microbursts are small bursts of traffic that are long enough to fill up the queue for the class but not sustained long enough for network management to see the bandwidth as high enough to tail drop. The first thing to try is to increase the queue limit for the class. You can make this change in real time without affecting forwarding traffic. Try doubling the queue limit and then monitor for drops. If you still observe drops, you can increase the queue limit again. Eventually the drops should become less frequent or stop altogether. During nonburst times, traffic will have the same behavior. During the microbursts, there will be periods of higher latency as packets drain from the deeper queue.{color}{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}By default, ASR1000 assigns a default queue-limit of 64 packets. {color}{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}Our first step would be to increase the queue-limit using dummy class and then check if the rate of drop decreases or not. Follow the below given steps, to increase the queue-limit of the default class. Kindly keep the queue-limit to 4096{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}class-map Default-level   <<< Dummy class{color}

{color:#1f4e79}match qos-group 20    <<< This group doesn't match anything, it is not configured to match ingress traffic.{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}policy-map \{name}{color}

{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}class Default-level  << First{color}

{color:#1f4e79}class class-default  << Second{color}

{color:#1f4e79}       queue-limit \{maximum} bytes{color}{color:#1f4e79} {color}

{color:#1f4e79}I would also like you to share the output of “show plat hard qfp act infra bqs que out def all” ( 5 times).{color}

column2095:
Feb 13, 2019 15:37;615b3e9cc5388b00696ca529;mail sent out to Rehan from Cisco TAC.

{color:#1f497d}Hi Rehan,{color}

{color:#1f497d} {color}

{color:#1f497d}I think we are good now, more than 18 hours without any total output drop.{color}

